course: dictionary
lesson: 4305
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Grit can mean tiny hard bits of stone, courage, or a way of spreading salt on icy roads. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it. «Grit or sand sprinkled on an icy path gives shoes more grip» is about the tiny hard bits, because sand and grip go together.
explain: «Salt from sea spray or from gritted winter roads makes iron rust faster» uses gritted as an action word. Roads are gritted, which means salt or grit is spread on them. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence, then try it in place of the word to check.
explain: When grit means courage, it is a quality a person has. In school, grit is praised as not giving up, practising again and bouncing back after a mistake. Phrases like «grit your teeth» show the same idea: you keep going even when something is hard.
fact: Grit can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action word).
fact: As a noun, grit means tiny hard bits of stone or sand, or courage and determination.
fact: As a verb, to grit means to spread grit or salt on icy roads so they are less slippery.
fact: «Birds have no teeth; a muscly part of the stomach called the gizzard grinds their food, often with swallowed grit.»
fact: To grit your teeth means to press your teeth together, or to bravely put up with something hard.
exercise: «Grit or sand sprinkled on an icy path gives shoes more grip.» What is the grit in this sentence? | A kind of bird | *Tiny hard bits of stone or sand | Courage to keep going | Cold, slippery ice
exercise: In school, grit is praised as a good quality. What does grit mean in that sense? | Eating slowly and carefully | Hiding in a quiet corner | *Not giving up when things are hard, and keeping going | Being cross with other people
exercise: «Birds have no teeth; a muscly part of the stomach called the gizzard grinds their food, often with swallowed grit.» What do birds swallow to help grind their food? | Feathers | Sticks | Water | *Tiny bits of stone or sand
test: «Salt from sea spray or from gritted winter roads makes iron rust faster.» In this sentence, gritted means | Shaken hard | Painted bright red | Washed clean | *Spread with grit or salt so the roads are less slippery
test: To grit your teeth means | *Press your teeth together and bravely put up with something hard | Brush your teeth very hard | Chew something crunchy | Smile for a photo
exam: Which sentence uses grit as a verb (an action word)? | Her grit got her through the race | Tiny grit stuck in my shoe | Grit is found in soil and sand | *The council gritted the icy road before dawn
